# Development Process (Gated PRs)

Every feature or fix follows the workflow in `docs/DEVELOPMENT_PROCESS.md` —
this rule is the PR-mechanics summary of that document, not a replacement.
Design ceremony is proportional to the change's risk-tier (see below); tests
are planned before production logic.

## Risk-Tier Policy

Every change is classified into a risk-tier before implementation —
**High** (four design artifacts), **Medium** (one concise design note;
the default for any non-High, non-Low change), or **Low** (design
artifacts exempt only). The **highest applicable tier wins**: a small
diff cannot be downgraded when it touches a high-tier path or invariant.
Record the chosen tier and rationale in the PR description.

- **High** (security/auth, agent identity or permissions, enforcement,
  migrations/schema/RLS, tenant or learning data, secrets or deployment
  boundaries, CI workflows, hooks; path globs include `src/*/security/**`,
  auth middleware, identity/scope dispatch, `enforcement/**`,
  `learning/tables.py` and its migrations, `.github/workflows/**`,
  `.cursor/hooks*`, deployment manifests, and secret configuration). High
  requires all four design artifacts before implementation:
  **architecture map**, **data-flow diagram**, **workflow states or
  wireframes**, and **threat model**. Primary layout:
  `docs/designs/<feature>/{ARCHITECTURE_MAP,DATA_FLOW,WORKFLOW_STATES,THREAT_MODEL}.md`.
- **Medium is the default** risk-tier for any non-High, non-Low change,
  including single-file behavior changes. Medium requires **one concise
  design note** covering architecture impact, data movement, failure
  behavior, risks, and planned tests.
- **Low** applies to documentation- or presentation-only work, test-only
  changes, and fixes under 20 gross changed lines measured as
  **additions plus deletions** (excluding mechanically generated
  artifacts) that do not touch high-tier paths or invariants. Low is
  **exempt from design artifacts** only. Branch isolation, CI, applicable
  tests, human ownership, and post-change review still apply.

## PR Mechanics

1. **Plan sign-off before code (proportional to risk-tier).** High
   changes get the four design artifacts reviewed by the relevant domain
   experts (security architect, SRE, template-DX) via subagents.
   Medium changes get a written plan with the one concise design note
   reviewed by the matching reviewer. Implementation starts only after
   every required reviewer returns APPROVED; CHANGES-REQUIRED verdicts
   are folded into the plan and re-reviewed. Low-tier changes may skip
   the design ceremony but not the branch, CI, tests, ownership, or
   review obligations above.
2. **One focused PR per change, on a branch.** Never commit directly to
   `main`.
3. **Validation before review.** `bash scripts/validate_generated.sh`
   must pass fully before requesting any review. Note the script exits 0
   even with warnings — read its output and treat warnings as review
   items, not noise. Test/check counts quoted in docs are measured from
   actual runs, never estimated.
4. **Post-implementation review of the actual diff.** Bugbot plus the
   matching domain expert review the branch. Findings are fixed with
   regression tests (a test that fails on the pre-fix code), then
   re-reviewed. Nothing is pushed or merged without an explicit final
   APPROVED. Every confirmed finding is then classified by a human
   maintainer as either a one-off defect or a recurring bug class; a
   recurring-class fix cannot receive final approval until the same PR
   ships a regression test, an update to the nearest relevant agent
   rule or instruction, and a register entry in
   `docs/BUG_CLASS_REGISTER.md` linking the source finding, invariant,
   rule change, and regression test. Agents may propose entries but
   cannot auto-classify, self-edit their own rules, or approve their
   own rule changes. See `docs/DEVELOPMENT_PROCESS.md` for the full
   contract.
5. **Strongest model only.** Implementation and review subagents run on
   the strongest available model. If a subagent silently falls back to
   a weaker model (e.g., on API limits), discard its output and redo
   it — or retro-audit anything already merged.

## Standing constraints

- **Detect, never auto-act:** shipped defaults may flag, log, or refuse
  explicitly (4xx) — they never silently alter prompts, truncate listings,
  or degrade responses. Enforcement beyond flags is opt-in via env flags,
  default off.
- **Honest docs:** every capability claim cites code and tests;
  limitations go in GOVERNANCE Non-Claims. Never overclaim.
- **Schema changes** go through the `MIGRATIONS` mechanism in
  `learning/tables.py` with the frozen v1 baseline (see the BASELINE
  FREEZE RULE in that file). Never add columns only to the live
  `TABLE_COLUMNS` baseline.
- **Line-cap headers are honest:** respect each module's stated cap; if
  a file must grow, update the header in the same PR rather than
  ignoring it.
- **No internal codenames:** never reference private
  inspiration/upstream project names in code, comments, docs, commits,
  or PR text. The `IP_PROTECTION_PATTERNS` list in
  `scripts/quick_checks.py` scans for proprietary references and can be
  extended with generic patterns, but the private names themselves are
  enforced by review — committing them to the pattern list would put
  them in the repo and defeat the purpose.
- **Template files that contain jinja placeholders (`{{ ... }}`) must
  have a `.jinja` suffix** or copier ships them unrendered.
- **Prompt reviewers are SHADOW-first.** A prompt reviewer
  (`.cursor/rules/*.mdc`, `.cursor/agents/*.md`) may recommend
  `BLOCK` only when its version is recorded as `BLOCKING` in
  `docs/REVIEWER_ASSURANCE.md`. Every prompt reviewer ships as
  `SHADOW`; promotion requires all vulnerable cases in the
  reviewer's declared domain detected, zero false blocking on safe
  cases, complete evidence, injection resistance, and recorded
  human approval — see `docs/REVIEWER_ASSURANCE.md` for the full
  protocol. A material change to a reviewer (prompt, scope, tools,
  or observed model behavior) returns it to SHADOW; behavior-neutral
  editorial changes are exempt. Prompt-reviewer runs are manual
  because no authenticated agent runner exists in CI.
